because the agency’s decision to withhold certain information under FOIA exemption 7(C) was proper. In determining whether disclosure of a particular type of document would constitute an unwarranted invasion of personal privacy, we consider two factors: the privacy interest that would be compromised by disclosure, and the public interest in disclosure. See Nat'l Archives and Records Admin. v. Favish, 541 U.S., at 172, 124 S.Ct., at *1591; Dep't of State v. Ray, 502 U.S, at 177, 112 S.Ct, at *1323. However, in this case, the parties have stipulated to the dismissal of this claim without prejudice. Turning now to plaintiff's claim that the FBI improperly withheld documents pursuant to FOIA exemptions 1 and 3, we find that the district court properly granted summary judgment in favor of the defendants, as there are no genuine issues of material fact regarding the applicability of these exemptions. See Gardels v. Cent. Intelligence Agency, 689 F.2dat 1100, 1103 (D.D.C.1982) (holding that the CIA properly invoked exemption 1 to withhold intelligence sources and methods), and McGehee v. Central Intelligence Agency. 697 F. 2d at *1236 n. 11 (same), aff'd, 711 F. Supp. 809 (D. D.C. 1989) (<HOLDING>);

A. holding that an agency's invocation of FOIA exemptions 1 and 3 requires a higher standard of proof of national security or intelligence gathering compromise when the documents withheld are not directly related to intelligence gathering methods. B. holding that all intelligence sources and methods are categorically exempt from disclosure under FOIA, without requiring a case-by-case determination of potential harm to national security or intelligence gathering capabilities. C. holding that a court must conduct an in camera review of the withheld documents to determine whether the agency properly invoked FOIA exemptions 1 and 3, rather than relying solely on the agency's affidavit. D. holding that the CIA may properly invoke FOIA exemptions 1 and 3 to withhold intelligence sources and methods, and documents related to such sources and methods, where the agency demonstrates that disclosure would compromise national security or intelligence gathering capabilities. E. holding that the CIA is only permitted to invoke FOIA exemptions 1 and 3 when the sources and methods withheld are the subject of an ongoing investigation, and disclosure would compromise the investigation's integrity.
